You are reviewing the repair verdicts queue on The Greatest Books, using the admin MCP server at <%= mcp_url %>. These are findings from the legacy Goodreads re-import. Each reimport_match says which book a reader's Goodreads row should be matched to. The finder or an AI proposed the match and flagged it for a person. The queue currently holds <%= pending_high %> high confidence, <%= pending_medium %> medium confidence and <%= pending_low %> low confidence proposed reimport_match verdicts. Work through all of them.

Use list_repair_verdicts with kind=reimport_match to list them (status defaults to proposed). Page through the whole list.

For each verdict:

1. Call get_repair_verdict. It returns the CSV facts for the row (title, author, ISBN and so on), the AI's answer, and the candidate books.
2. Compare the facts with each candidate book. Use your own web search when the records don't settle it.
3. If the AI's answer is right, call approve_repair_verdict.
4. If the AI's answer is wrong, call correct_repair_verdict with the id of the right book. If none of the candidate books is the work, call it with create: true to make a new book.

Never call reject_repair_verdict on a reimport_match. A rejected match is final and its rows wait. Correct it instead.

Use bulk_approve_repair_verdicts only for verdicts you have each read with get_repair_verdict. Do not approve a whole page unread.

Approving or correcting does not change anything at once. The next books:goodreads_reimport:apply run acts on it, so treat each answer as final.

You are authorised to act without asking me. If you genuinely cannot tell, skip the verdict and say why.

When you finish, report what you did with each verdict: its id, what you did (approved, corrected to a book id, corrected to a new book, skipped) and one line of reasoning.
